Absolutely! Here’s how you can create an input field with a clickable SVG glass (search) icon on the right side. When the icon is clicked (after typing something in the input), a function will be called.

## **HTML + SVG + JS Example**

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>Input with Search Icon</title>
  <style>
    .input-container {
      position: relative;
      width: 250px;
    }

    .input-container input {
      width: 100%;
      padding-right: 38px; /* Make space for the icon */
      height: 36px;
      box-sizing: border-box;
      font-size: 16px;
    }

    .input-container .search-icon {
      position: absolute;
      right: 8px;
      top: 50%;
      transform: translateY(-50%);
      width: 22px;
      height: 22px;
      cursor: pointer;
      fill: #666;
      transition: fill 0.2s;
    }

    .input-container .search-icon:hover {
      fill: #222;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <div class="input-container">
    <input
      id="searchInput"
      type="text"
      placeholder="Search..."
      autocomplete="off"
    />
    <svg
      class="search-icon"
      id="searchIcon"
      xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
      viewBox="0 0 24 24"
    >
      <circle cx="11" cy="11" r="7" stroke="#333" stroke-width="2" fill="none"/>
      <line x1="16.5" y1="16.5" x2="21" y2="21" stroke="#333" stroke-width="2"/>
    </svg>
  </div>
  <script>
    document.getElementById('searchIcon').addEventListener('click', function() {
      const value = document.getElementById('searchInput').value;
      if(value.trim()!=="") {
        // Replace with your function:
        alert("Searching for: " + value);
      }
    });
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

---

## **How it works**

- The input and SVG are wrapped in a `div` with `position: relative`.  
- The input has extra right padding to make room for the icon.
- The SVG search icon (glass) is absolutely positioned on the right.
- The SVG is clickable and triggers a JS function using `addEventListener('click', ...)`
- If the input is not empty, the function runs (replace `alert` with your function).

